@page "/privacy"
@namespace Ben.Web.Website.Library.Support
@inject Microsoft.Extensions.Options.IOptions<Ben.Data.Common.SiteIdentity> SiteOptions

@* Anonymous on purpose, and the reason is not only courtesy: App Store Connect requires a privacy
   policy URL that Apple's reviewer can open WITHOUT an account, and so does Google. A page behind
   a sign-in is a rejected submission. Nothing here reads the signed-in user — see
   feedback_author_sees_what_visitor_cannot for the class of bug that hides in pages like this. *@

<PageTitle>Privacy — @SiteOptions.Value.Name</PageTitle>

<div class="container-fluid px-3 py-4" style="max-width:860px; margin:0 auto;">

    <h1 class="mb-1">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="text-secondary mb-4">
        How @SiteOptions.Value.Name and the IsHaunted iPhone and iPad app handle your information.
        Last updated 10/19/2026.
    </p>

    <div class="card border-primary mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">
            <h2 class="h5 card-title mb-2">The short version</h2>
            <p class="mb-0">
                We collect nothing about you except what you give us directly. There is no data
                broker and no advertising inside the site or the app. The one exception is on this
                website: if you say yes to our cookie question, Google measures whether you came
                from one of our ads and went on to subscribe. If you say no, or never answer,
                nothing from Google loads. The iPhone and iPad app sends nothing to Google or any
                other measurement service.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">What you give us</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Your account.</strong> An email address, a display name and an @@name. You may
            add a real name, a profile photo, a birth year and contact details — every one of those
            is optional and you control who can see it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>What you create.</strong> Cases, investigations, events, equipment records,
            notes, reports, messages, feed posts and comments — the substance of using the site.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Evidence you upload.</strong> Photos, video, audio and documents, together with
            whatever you write about them.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Field sessions from the app.</strong> Sensor readings, photos, audio and
            location, recorded on your device while a session is running.
        </li>
        @* Said here from 2026-09-19, when Microsoft sign-ins began being recorded alongside the
           password and Apple ones that already were. The page had never mentioned the category at
           all, and extending what is kept is the moment to describe it rather than the moment to
           keep quiet. Deliberately specific about what is NOT kept, because that is the part
           somebody actually wants to know — and it is the rule the table itself is built on. *@
        <li>
            <strong>When you sign in.</strong> We record that you signed in, when, and which way
            in — a password, Apple, or Microsoft. We do not record your IP address, your device or
            where you were.
        </li>
        @* From the iPhone and iPad app 1.1.0 (item 252), declared in the App Store as Device ID. *@
        <li>
            <strong>Notifications from the app.</strong> If you allow notifications on your iPhone or
            iPad, we keep the address Apple gives that phone for them while you are signed in on it,
            so your group can tell you its session has started or that you have been let in. It is
            used for nothing else, is removed when you sign out, and is forgotten when Apple tells us
            the phone no longer has the app.
        </li>
        @* From 10/02/2026 (item 253): EVP scans and review choices kept to improve the scanner.
           Said here the day it began, like the sign-in record above, and specific about what is
           not kept. *@
        <li>
            <strong>EVP scans and your review choices.</strong> When you scan a recording for EVP,
            we keep a few measurements of each sound the scan finds, and whether you kept or dismissed
            each one and played it first, to improve how the scanner ranks what it finds. No audio is
            copied, nothing records who made the choice, and they are deleted with the recording.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Location, specifically</h2>
    <p>
        The app stamps readings with your location <em>only</em> while a field session is running,
        and only if you granted location permission. Those readings stay on your device unless you
        choose to upload the session to your group. Nothing tracks your location in the background,
        and the app never asks for always-on location.
    </p>

    @* Added 10/19/2026 with the Google Ads tag (AdsConsent.razor, ads-consent.js). Keep it true to
       the code: nothing loads before Accept, and the apps send nothing. *@
    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Measuring our ads, on the website only</h2>
    <p>
        We advertise on Google. To know whether those ads lead anyone to subscribe, this website
        asks the first time you visit whether we may measure our ads. Only if you choose Accept does
        your browser load Google's ad tag. Google may then set cookies and learn which page you
        visited, that you came from one of our ads, and, when a group's subscription goes through,
        that a subscription happened. We do not send Google your name, email, group, or anything
        you have written or uploaded. Google handles what it receives under its own privacy policy.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you choose Decline, or do not answer, nothing from Google loads and nothing is measured.
        Your choice is kept in your browser. You can change it at any time with
        <strong>Cookie settings</strong> at the bottom of every page; clearing your browser's site
        data also clears it, and we ask again.
    </p>
    <p>
        This is about the website only. The IsHaunted iPhone and iPad app does none of this.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">What we do not do</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>We do not sell or rent your information, ever.</li>
        <li>We do not show advertising, and nothing here profiles you for anyone else's.</li>
        <li>We use no analytics, telemetry or crash-reporting service. The only measurement is the
            ad measurement above, on the website, and only if you accept it.</li>
        <li>We do not track you across other apps or websites.</li>
        <li>We do not buy information about you from anybody.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Who else touches it</h2>
    <p>
        A short list, each for one job:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Apple and Microsoft</strong> — only if you choose Sign in with Apple or a Microsoft account. They tell us you signed in; we tell them nothing about you.</li>
        <li><strong>Our mail provider</strong> — to deliver the email the site sends you.</li>
        <li><strong>Google</strong> — on the website only, and only if you accept ad measurement. See above.</li>
        <li><strong>Geocod.io</strong> — turns an address you type into map coordinates. It receives the address, not your identity.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Who can see what you post</h2>
    <p>
        You decide, per item. Most things start private to you and the group you share them with.
        Cases marked as private engagements have client names and addresses hidden from everyone
        outside the group, including in anything published publicly. Anything you deliberately
        publish — a public case page, a feed post, a group profile — is public, which is the point
        of publishing it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">How long we keep it</h2>
    <p>
        For as long as your account exists. Delete an item and it goes; close your account and we
        remove your personal details and the content attached to you.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Security</h2>
    <p>
        Traffic runs over HTTPS. Passwords are stored hashed and never in readable form, and you can
        turn on two-factor authentication in your account settings.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Children</h2>
    <p>
        @SiteOptions.Value.Name is not intended for children under 13, and we do not knowingly
        collect information from them.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Your choices</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Edit or delete what you have posted, at any time, from the item itself.</li>
        <li>Change what your profile shows other people in your account settings.</li>
        <li>Accept or decline ad measurement on the website from Cookie settings at the bottom of any page.</li>
        <li>Ask us for a copy of your information, or ask us to delete your account.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Asking us anything</h2>
    <p class="mb-4">
        Use the <a href="/contact">contact form</a> — no account needed — for any question about
        this page, for a copy of your information, or to have your account removed.
    </p>

</div>
